================================================================================
LINUX FILE SYSTEM & CORE COMMANDS NOTES
================================================================================

PART 1: LINUX DIRECTORY HIERARCHY (WHAT LIVES WHERE)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/ (Root)
- The starting point of the entire Linux filesystem hierarchy.
- No drive letters (C:, D:) exist; all partitions and devices branch from here.

/home
- Workspaces for standard non-root users (e.g., /home/username).
- Houses user files, configurations, and personal data.

/root
- Dedicated home directory of the system administrator (root user).
- Kept on the root partition so admin access functions even if /home fails to mount.

/bin (Binaries)
- Core command-line executables required for basic operation and recovery.
- Examples: ls, cp, mv, rm, cat, mkdir.

/sbin (System Binaries)
- Administrative management utilities intended for the root user.
- Examples: fdisk, iptables, reboot, ip.

/usr (Unix System Resources)
- Secondary software library containing applications, utilities, and assets.
- Houses non-critical user-space programs (/usr/bin), documentation, and tools.

/lib
- Core shared libraries and kernel modules required by binaries in /bin and /sbin.
- Equivalent to .dll files in Windows; eliminates redundant code across programs.

/etc
- Central directory for system-wide and service configuration files.
- Examples: network settings, SSH configurations (/etc/ssh), DNS (/etc/resolv.conf).

/var (Variable)
- Dynamic, constantly changing runtime data.
- Stores system logs (/var/log), databases, print spools, and temporary caches.

/tmp (Temporary)
- Scratch storage for temporary files created by applications.
- Cleared periodically or completely wiped upon system reboot.

/dev (Devices)
- Hardware device nodes exposed as files ("everything is a file").
- Examples: /dev/sda (hard disk), /dev/null (data discard node).

/proc (Processes)
- In-memory virtual pseudo-filesystem created by the Linux kernel.
- Consumes 0 bytes on disk; provides live system stats, PID metrics, and CPU data.

/boot
- Static files required to boot the machine.
- Contains the compiled Linux kernel (vmlinuz), initramfs, and GRUB files.

/media & /mnt
- /media: Automatic mount directory for removable drives (USB, external disks).
- /mnt: Manual mount point for administrators attaching disks or network shares.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 2: COMMAND COMPARISONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. rmdir vs. rm -rf

Command: rmdir <directory>
- Purpose: Removes empty directories only.
- Mechanism: Makes an rmdir() system call. Fails immediately if any file, 
  subfolder, or hidden dotfile exists inside.
- Error when not empty: "Directory not empty".
- Safety: Completely safe against accidental data deletion.

Command: rm -rf <directory>
- Purpose: Recursively and forcefully deletes directories and all their contents.
- Flags:
  -r: Recursive traversal (descends into every child directory and file).
  -f: Force (ignores non-existent files and bypasses confirmation prompts).
- Mechanism: Unlinks every child file inode and removes directories bottom-up.
- Risk: Extremely dangerous if run with typos or unquoted shell variables 
  (e.g., rm -rf /path /to/dir will target root).


2. Vim Exit Commands: :wq vs. :q vs. :q! vs. :x

Command: :q (Quit)
- Action: Closes the current editor buffer and exits.
- Safety: Fails and throws "E37: No write since last change" if modifications 
  were made without saving.

Command: :wq (Write and Quit)
- Action: Saves (writes) memory buffer changes to disk, then exits Vim.
- Note: Always updates the file's modification timestamp (mtime), even if no 
  actual edits took place. Fails if opened without write permissions.

Command: :q! (Force Quit / Discard)
- Action: Exits immediately without saving.
- Discards all unsaved edits made during the current editing session.

Command: :x (Smart Exit)
- Action: Writes only if modifications exist, then exits.
- Difference from :wq: If you only viewed the file without changes, :x does not 
  update the file timestamp.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 3: QUICK CHEAT SHEET
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Directory   | Primary Purpose
----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------
/           | Top-level root of the whole filesystem
/home       | Standard user workspaces
/root       | Superuser administrator home directory
/bin        | Essential system command binaries (ls, cp, rm)
/sbin       | Administrative system tools (fdisk, reboot)
/usr        | Software library and installed packages (/usr/bin)
/etc        | System and daemon configuration text files
/lib        | Shared libraries used by executables (.so files)
/var        | Variable growing data and logs (/var/log)
/tmp        | Ephemeral scratch files (purged on reboot)
/dev        | Hardware device representation nodes
/proc       | Live in-memory kernel/process statistics
/boot       | Kernel binaries and bootloader files
/media      | Automatic mount point for removable storage
/mnt        | Manual mount point for disks and network shares

Command     | Behavior
----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------
rmdir dir   | Deletes 'dir' ONLY if completely empty (fails if files exist)
rm -rf dir  | Forcibly and recursively wipes 'dir' and all contents inside
:q          | Quits Vim (refuses if unsaved edits exist)
:wq         | Writes changes to disk, updates mtime, and quits Vim
:q!         | Quits Vim immediately, discarding all unsaved edits
:x          | Writes only if changes exist, then quits
